  "query_preview": ".. (Tuesday through Friday)\nRelief will be short-lived as the strong upper-level ridge is \nprogged to expand back eastward into the Midwest by Tuesday and \nWednesday. This will trigger a resurgence of widespread \n90-degree temperatures, with triple-digit heat index values once\nagain possible.\n\nBy mid-week, a closed upper-level low over the Pacific \nNorthwest will open and advance east across the Canadian \nPrairies, buckling the upper ridge. In response, a surface low \nwill deepen as it lifts across Manitoba on Tuesday and toward \nHudson Bay on Wednesday. The trailing cold front will sag \nsouthward into the Upper Midwest, with its southern tail / col\narea eventually pushing into central Illinois on Wednesday. \nPrecipitation chances with this frontal passage remain quite low\n(10-20%) due to weak convergence and the displacement of the \nprimary upper-level support far to the north.\n\nChances for more substantial rainfall increase (40-50%) \nThursday night into Friday morning. This is in response to a \nstrengthening low-level jet to our west, which will drive \nrenewed thunderstorm development along the stalling frontal \nboundary.",
